So the case-frame structure itself was legal.\n- **98-2 and 98-3 both failed at their self-test**, so no stage run was launched.\n  - Deleting only the 9 unconnected wires did not make the copy runnable after the first move.\n  - Clearing all broken wires in memory after that move did not work either.\n  - I had assumed, without measuring it, that the copy should be runnable after that one move. The retrospective named this as my fault (`inference-over-measurement`), and I accepted it.\n- **98-4 was blocked, correctly.** Your PD210 reached the plan while the cycle was running: the plot goes into a separate display loop fed by a local variable, and the fgate work is dropped. The material session read that and ran nothing.\n\n**What is left behind**\n- `move_into_frame` keeps its cycle-98 change. It refuses to act unless the VI is runnable before the move, and it raises if a move leaves broken wires. Its only user was the dropped fgate stage. Any later design that uses it on a copy that is broken by design must say how it gets past that check.\n- The cycle-98 facts are kept in the plan as PD211(b), for any step that moves an existing terminal.\n- The broken intermediate stays on disk, since nothing needs it deleted. The originals and `D1_s1_copy.vi` (md5 `3e3d23ce…`) are unchanged, and the material sessions reported LabVIEW closed after each run.\n\n**Cycle 99 starts with** PD210(f) and (d), in `docs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md`. First comes a read-only card that collects facts for the display loop:\n- where the plot's data comes from;\n- what writes each plot indicator;\n- the existing stop signal;\n- each plot's per-frame cost;\n- the cost of writing the plot array to a local variable.\n\nThen judgement writes the design page. `tools/bench/next.json` and the NEXT section of `STATUS.md` both say this.\n\n**For you:** D-2026-09-26-02 is answered by PD210. The tooling card with four small repairs is still owed. It is queued behind the display-loop work.","ttft_ms":1961,